WEBVTT

00:04.400 --> 00:07.657
Welcome to FlowSense, from Environmental Data Services.

00:07.657 --> 00:12.277
It puts your whole sewer network in one place, from the reading in the chamber

00:12.277 --> 00:16.660
to the decision it pays for, so problems are found early and fixed sooner.

00:17.060 --> 00:19.775
It does the work of four separate purchases:

00:19.775 --> 00:24.466
the logger portal, the yearly infiltration study, a modelling engagement and

00:24.466 --> 00:25.330
the reporting.

00:25.330 --> 00:30.020
All four read one validated record, so nobody retypes a number between them.

00:30.420 --> 00:33.369
Sign in with the details your administrator gave you.

00:33.369 --> 00:37.710
Optional two-factor authentication adds a code from your authenticator app, so

00:37.710 --> 00:38.990
your data stays secure.

00:39.390 --> 00:42.832
This demonstration uses Riverton, a fictional council.

00:42.832 --> 00:45.700
Every name and figure you see is sample data.

00:46.100 --> 00:49.502
The menu on the left follows the way your team works:

00:49.502 --> 00:53.867
monitor, maintain, analyse, engineer and deliver, then reference and

00:53.867 --> 00:54.830
administration.

00:55.230 --> 00:59.262
You only see the rooms your account includes and your role allows, so nothing

00:59.262 --> 01:00.100
gets in the way.

01:00.500 --> 01:03.698
Press Control K to jump straight to anything.

01:03.698 --> 01:06.470
Type what you want, not where it lives.

01:07.170 --> 01:09.674
Every page has a Guide link, top right.

01:09.674 --> 01:12.820
It opens help on exactly what you are looking at.

01:13.520 --> 01:15.640
Settings holds what is yours:

01:15.640 --> 01:19.440
time zone, light or dark theme, password and alerts.

01:20.240 --> 01:22.939
Network Overview answers one question:

01:22.939 --> 01:23.934
what is wrong?

01:23.934 --> 01:29.475
The worst problem always sits at the top, and when all is well, the page stays

01:29.475 --> 01:29.830
calm.

01:30.230 --> 01:32.940
Since you last looked catches you up in seconds.

01:33.340 --> 01:38.062
Every site is judged against its own usual for that hour, learned from its own

01:38.062 --> 01:42.662
history, so a busy trunk main and a quiet branch line are each held to their

01:42.662 --> 01:43.328
own normal.

01:43.328 --> 01:45.568
The status bar counts what needs you:

01:45.568 --> 01:48.837
alarms, offline sites, telemetry health and batteries.

01:48.837 --> 01:51.500
Select any figure to see the list behind it.

01:51.900 --> 01:53.966
Needs attention gives the detail.

01:53.966 --> 01:58.285
Here, two sites are breaching an alarm and one has stopped reporting.

01:58.285 --> 02:02.730
Each row gives the site, the reason, and how long it has been that way.

02:03.130 --> 02:06.515
The map shows every site, coloured by its worst state.

02:06.515 --> 02:09.650
Point at a row, and its site is ringed on the map.

02:10.050 --> 02:12.570
Layers filters the map by site type.

02:12.570 --> 02:16.350
Weather lays rain radar or satellite cloud underneath.

02:16.750 --> 02:21.208
Further down sit the 72 hour rain forecast, anything Blockage Watch has

02:21.208 --> 02:25.478
flagged, the last 30 days against the 30 before, and what inflow and

02:25.478 --> 02:27.550
infiltration costs you each year.

02:27.950 --> 02:29.436
Make the page your own.

02:29.436 --> 02:34.411
The grid button lets you move, resize or hide panels, and your layout follows

02:34.411 --> 02:35.380
you everywhere.

02:35.780 --> 02:40.290
Sites lists every site on the account, searchable by name or logger.

02:40.290 --> 02:43.540
Filter, sort, and switch between table and cards.

02:43.940 --> 02:45.952
Open a site for the full picture.

02:45.952 --> 02:50.526
Tiles set each latest reading against this time yesterday, so changes stand

02:50.526 --> 02:50.770
out.

02:51.170 --> 02:53.674
Pick a period and the charts redraw.

02:53.674 --> 02:56.179
Here is Barrack Street over 30 days.

02:56.179 --> 03:00.491
The flow is steady, but the depth has been climbing for weeks.

03:00.491 --> 03:02.300
We will come back to that.

03:02.700 --> 03:05.396
Click the legend to hide or show a channel.

03:05.396 --> 03:09.220
Compare adds a second site, and Channels picks what is drawn.

03:09.620 --> 03:12.754
Export CSV gives you the readings behind the chart.

03:12.754 --> 03:17.426
For modelling saves the same window as a file for InfoWorks ICM, EPA SWMM or

03:17.426 --> 03:20.560
MIKE plus, so your modellers start from clean data.

03:21.260 --> 03:26.044
The tabs hold diagnostics, inflow and infiltration, alarms, and the crew's

03:26.044 --> 03:27.143
notes and photos:

03:27.143 --> 03:29.470
the site's whole story in one place.

03:29.870 --> 03:32.657
Fleet Health is for the crew leaving the depot:

03:32.657 --> 03:35.800
every logger, worst first, with the reason beside it.

03:36.200 --> 03:38.024
Mulgowie Road has gone silent.

03:38.024 --> 03:42.339
Tarampa Street's battery is fading, and FlowSense fits its discharge to

03:42.339 --> 03:45.500
predict when to replace it, before any data is lost.

03:45.900 --> 03:48.338
Visit plan turns the list into trips:

03:48.338 --> 03:52.490
one visit per site, grouped by area, to save wasted kilometres.

03:52.890 --> 03:55.602
Alarms shows what nobody has picked up yet.

03:55.602 --> 04:00.395
Breaches come first, and an alarm that clears itself stays until someone has

04:00.395 --> 04:00.900
seen it.

04:01.300 --> 04:04.672
Each row shows what tripped, the reading and when.

04:04.672 --> 04:07.775
Check the channel around it, then acknowledge.

04:07.775 --> 04:10.810
Your name and the time are logged against it.

04:11.210 --> 04:14.741
Alarm rules decide who is told, by email or text.

04:14.741 --> 04:19.570
The bell on every page counts what is waiting, so nothing slips by.

04:19.970 --> 04:24.917
Blockage Watch spots pipes silting or ragging up, weeks before they overflow.

04:24.917 --> 04:29.478
It reads each site's overnight level on dry days only, so rain is never

04:29.478 --> 04:31.020
mistaken for a blockage.

04:31.420 --> 04:33.531
Here is the climb at Barrack Street.

04:33.531 --> 04:37.460
You get the verdict first, silt or grease, and what to do about it.

04:37.860 --> 04:39.004
Then the numbers:

04:39.004 --> 04:44.184
how far the level has risen, how fast, and roughly how long until the pipe is

04:44.184 --> 04:44.520
full.

04:44.520 --> 04:49.297
Caught here, it is a planned clean, not an overflow and a report to the

04:49.297 --> 04:49.970
regulator.

04:50.370 --> 04:51.939
Each flag opens a case.

04:51.939 --> 04:56.989
Assign it to your team, or send any crew an inspection link, with no login

04:56.989 --> 04:57.466
needed.

04:57.466 --> 05:00.810
The case closes itself once the site stays clear.

05:01.210 --> 05:05.554
Underneath is every day behind the verdict, including the ones set aside, so

05:05.554 --> 05:07.040
the call is easy to check.

05:07.440 --> 05:11.368
PSM, the Pump Station Manager, turns a wet well into a flow meter.

05:11.368 --> 05:16.011
With a known plan area, the level between pump starts measures the inflow, and

05:16.011 --> 05:20.654
the drawdown measures what was pumped, so you get flow data without installing

05:20.654 --> 05:21.130
a meter.

05:21.530 --> 05:25.433
The top line shows what needs attention across your stations.

05:25.433 --> 05:28.120
Change switches station, worst news first.

05:28.520 --> 05:30.791
The drawing shows the well to scale:

05:30.791 --> 05:35.205
the water level now, the pump start and stop, and what is arriving and

05:35.205 --> 05:35.710
leaving.

05:36.110 --> 05:37.626
Each pump has a card:

05:37.626 --> 05:43.042
what it is actually pumping, a dial against its rating, and 90 days against

05:43.042 --> 05:44.270
its own baseline.

05:44.670 --> 05:47.309
Below is the full record for the period:

05:47.309 --> 05:51.928
inflow and outflow, volumes, the typical day, pump condition, cycling,

05:51.928 --> 05:53.710
overflows and data quality.

05:54.110 --> 05:58.797
Pump condition flags a pump losing output since its last service, and ranks

05:58.797 --> 06:01.860
the likely cause, so you can act before it fails.

06:01.860 --> 06:04.860
Fleet table compares every station side by side.

06:05.560 --> 06:07.625
Trade Waste has a room of its own.

06:07.625 --> 06:09.083
Today shows what is due:

06:09.083 --> 06:13.699
overdue inspections, lapsing approvals, and breaches found by the meters and

06:13.699 --> 06:14.610
the laboratory.

06:15.010 --> 06:18.640
Nothing counts against a business until an officer confirms it.

06:19.040 --> 06:23.643
Monitoring checks each metered business against its approval, probe by probe.

06:23.643 --> 06:28.306
The register keeps each business and its whole compliance history, and Charges

06:28.306 --> 06:30.040
works out what each one owes.

06:30.740 --> 06:34.361
The Assets room keeps everything done to your pipes:

06:34.361 --> 06:39.513
CCTV grades, relines, repairs, chokes and overflows, each against your own

06:39.513 --> 06:40.140
asset ID.

06:40.540 --> 06:44.055
The map colours each main by risk, worst listed first.

06:44.055 --> 06:47.700
Works tracks each job from approval through to finished.

06:48.400 --> 06:52.948
Every job says what it changes on the risk register, and Benefit adds the

06:52.948 --> 06:53.758
programme up.

06:53.758 --> 06:58.369
Here, the four finished jobs left one fewer main at high risk, and came in

06:58.369 --> 06:59.740
under their estimates.

07:00.440 --> 07:03.810
A series of eight short videos covers this room in full.

07:04.210 --> 07:07.225
Flow Analytics takes a close look at one site.

07:07.225 --> 07:11.550
Choose a site and a period, and every tab answers for that choice.

07:11.950 --> 07:16.130
The main chart draws every channel together, with anomalies, thresholds,

07:16.130 --> 07:18.220
calibrations and wet weather marked.

07:18.620 --> 07:22.890
Below it, Insights lists what the platform noticed without being asked.

07:22.890 --> 07:26.860
Treat them as leads, and confirm each in the tab that measures it.

07:27.260 --> 07:29.955
Scattergraph plots depth against velocity.

07:29.955 --> 07:34.897
A healthy pipe traces a tight band, while silt, surcharge and a ragged sensor

07:34.897 --> 07:36.630
each leave their own shape.

07:37.230 --> 07:40.820
Quality shows how complete the record is, before you quote it.

07:41.520 --> 07:46.418
I/I separates what the rain added from the dry weather pattern, in kilolitres,

07:46.418 --> 07:47.360
storm by storm.

07:48.060 --> 07:51.603
Balance checks downstream flow against what left upstream.

07:51.603 --> 07:55.390
Compare puts several sites on one chart, over the same period.

07:56.190 --> 07:59.870
Engineering brings five tools together along the top of the page:

07:59.870 --> 08:04.060
work that usually means a consultancy engagement, in a workspace you keep.

08:04.460 --> 08:09.769
GIS Check comes first, because every engineering tool relies on the GIS.

08:09.769 --> 08:15.520
It lists what is missing, catchment by catchment, most important first, with a

08:15.520 --> 08:17.437
tested guess for each gap.

08:17.437 --> 08:20.460
Nothing is ever written back to your GIS.

08:20.860 --> 08:25.752
Asset Register tests your GIS against itself, and against the flow meters.

08:25.752 --> 08:30.909
Every finding names the asset, the record and the evidence that disagrees, and

08:30.909 --> 08:33.620
can be raised as a works job in one step.

08:34.020 --> 08:37.542
Hydraulic Modelling takes a project through four phases:

08:37.542 --> 08:40.435
review the data, calibrate, model and deliver.

08:40.435 --> 08:45.090
Progress is read from what the project holds, not from which tabs you have

08:45.090 --> 08:45.530
opened.

08:45.930 --> 08:50.943
Calibrate fits each site's response to rain, then tests it against storms it

08:50.943 --> 08:55.230
has not seen, scored on the CIWEM criteria modellers already use.

08:55.830 --> 09:00.424
Model runs a full dynamic wave simulation of your pipes, right in the browser.

09:00.424 --> 09:04.841
The platform does every step the data can settle, and stops only where your

09:04.841 --> 09:06.019
judgement is needed:

09:06.019 --> 09:07.020
the design storm.

09:07.720 --> 09:12.500
Each run checks its water balance, then shows where pipes surcharge and flood.

09:12.500 --> 09:16.790
A long section shows the water level down any run of pipes, manhole to

09:16.790 --> 09:17.280
outfall.

09:17.980 --> 09:20.866
Deliver turns the model into a programme.

09:20.866 --> 09:26.074
Enter your rehabilitation options, and FlowSense ranks them by the cost of

09:26.074 --> 09:31.423
each kilolitre of I/I they remove, so the budget funds the best value first.

09:31.423 --> 09:36.842
Results and observed data export for InfoWorks ICM, EPA SWMM or MIKE plus, so

09:36.842 --> 09:38.320
the work stays yours.

09:39.020 --> 09:43.454
The I/I Heat Map paints severity along the pipes draining to each meter,

09:43.454 --> 09:46.595
following the network, not straight lines on a map.

09:46.595 --> 09:49.120
A pipe with no meter below it stays grey.

09:49.720 --> 09:54.706
Inspection priority tells crews which mains to look at first, so CCTV money

09:54.706 --> 09:57.033
goes where the water is getting in.

09:57.033 --> 10:00.690
Export takes the map away for a report or your own GIS.

10:01.090 --> 10:06.210
Data Scrubbing finds spikes, flat lines and drift, and proposes a repair for

10:06.210 --> 10:06.547
each.

10:06.547 --> 10:09.040
Nothing changes until you approve it.

10:09.440 --> 10:11.596
One builder makes every report.

10:11.596 --> 10:15.490
Choose the type, the sites, the period and the sections.

10:15.890 --> 10:18.980
The preview is the document, so what you see is what is sent.

10:19.880 --> 10:25.022
Export a branded PDF with your logo, or a CSV bundle for anyone who wants the

10:25.022 --> 10:25.557
numbers.

10:25.557 --> 10:29.430
Schedule it once, and it reaches the right people on time.

10:29.830 --> 10:35.899
Data Export feeds your other systems on a schedule, over SFTP, FTPS or MQTT.

10:35.899 --> 10:40.770
Each run sends only what is new since the last good delivery.

10:41.170 --> 10:46.447
Any figure FlowSense calculates, rather than measures, carries a note.

10:46.447 --> 10:48.557
This page explains each one:

10:48.557 --> 10:53.909
what it reads, the steps, the values used, and what it cannot tell you.

10:53.909 --> 10:55.040
No black boxes.

10:55.440 --> 10:59.385
The notes are generated from the software itself, so they never fall out of

10:59.385 --> 11:00.752
step with the calculation.

11:00.752 --> 11:04.855
It also lists the published standards FlowSense is built to, and its own tests

11:04.855 --> 11:07.800
fail the build if the software stops agreeing with them.

11:08.200 --> 11:12.378
Data Assurance records how much of the data due from each site actually

11:12.378 --> 11:13.791
arrived, month by month.

11:13.791 --> 11:17.910
Each month is locked once it closes, so the record stands up to audit.

11:18.310 --> 11:21.241
The User Guide has a short article for every screen.

11:21.241 --> 11:24.960
It is searchable, and covers only the parts of FlowSense you have.

11:25.360 --> 11:27.472
Admin holds what runs the account.

11:27.472 --> 11:30.330
You only see the sections you have been given.

11:30.730 --> 11:34.780
Team is where you add people, and choose which sites and channels they can

11:34.780 --> 11:34.999
see.

11:34.999 --> 11:38.940
There are no per user fees, so everyone who needs FlowSense can have it.

11:39.340 --> 11:42.646
Add Site connects a new site to whatever feeds it:

11:42.646 --> 11:47.804
an EDS logger, another brand you already own, your existing SCADA system, or a

11:47.804 --> 11:48.134
file.

11:48.134 --> 11:51.110
Nothing is installed on your control network.

11:51.510 --> 11:54.970
Alarm rules set the thresholds, who is told, and how often.

11:55.370 --> 12:00.206
Channel setup names each channel once, and that name follows it into every

12:00.206 --> 12:01.840
chart, export and report.

12:02.240 --> 12:05.861
Branding puts your logo in the app and on your reports.

12:05.861 --> 12:09.680
An API and webhooks connect FlowSense to your own systems.

12:10.080 --> 12:14.329
Field Mode puts FlowSense on a phone, right at the manhole.

12:14.329 --> 12:18.001
It is built for one hand, in gloves, in bright sun.

12:18.001 --> 12:21.170
Find a site by name, or whatever is nearest.

12:21.570 --> 12:26.043
Check the logger and the level, then note what you saw, with photos.

12:26.043 --> 12:26.898
No reception?

12:26.898 --> 12:30.450
It saves anyway, and sends itself when signal returns.

12:30.850 --> 12:32.003
That is FlowSense:

12:32.003 --> 12:36.743
one record, from the chamber to the capital plan, with documented methods,

12:36.743 --> 12:39.946
published standards and tests behind every screen.

12:39.946 --> 12:42.060
Built and supported in Australia.

12:42.460 --> 12:46.524
Start each morning on Network Overview, and follow each question to the room

12:46.524 --> 12:47.380
that answers it.

12:47.780 --> 12:52.971
The Guide link is on every page, and Found a bug in Settings sends our team a

12:52.971 --> 12:54.925
screenshot, so help is quick.

12:54.925 --> 12:58.970
To see FlowSense on your own network, get in touch with EDS.
